Spring Framework :
A framework that manages Java objects and reduces development effort.

Bean :
A Bean is simply An object created and managed by Spring.
example :@Component
         public class Student {}

IOC (Inversion Of Control) :
--> The responsibility of creating objects is transferred from the programmer to the Spring Container.
--> IOC means Spring takes responsibility for creating and managing objects instead of the programmer creating them manually.

Container : 
--> Spring Container is like a factory.
--> Spring Container is responsible for creating, managing, and providing Spring Beans.
Types of Spring Container :
BeanFactory --> Basic container.
ApplicationContext --> Advanced container.

Spring Container
       |
       |
Creates Objects
       |
       |
Stores Objects
       |
       |
Provides Objects

ApplicationContext :
ApplicationContext is the Spring Container that creates and manages Spring Beans.

Dependency Injection (DI) :
Dependency Injection means providing required objects (dependencies)
to a class instead of letting the class create them itself.

Types of Dependency Injection :

1. Constructor Injection :
Dependency is injected through the constructor.

Example :
@Component
public class Car 
{
    private Engine engine;
    @Autowired
    public Car(Engine engine) 
    {
        this.engine = engine;
    }
    public void drive() 
    {
        engine.start();
    }
}

Example2 :
//Car class
package com.teju.di;
public class Car 
{
    Engine engine; // Car needs an Engine
    public Car(Engine engine) 
    {
        this.engine = engine; // Store the received Engine inside the Car
    }
    public void drive() 
    {
        engine.start(); // Ask the Engine to start
        System.out.println("Car Running"); // Display that the Car is running
    }
}

//Engine Class
package com.teju.di;
public class Engine 
{
    public void start() 
    {
        System.out.println("Engine Started");
    }
    public void stop() 
    {
        System.out.println("Engine Stopped");
    }
    public void breakdown() 
    {
        System.out.println("Engine breakdown");
    }
}

//Main class
package com.teju.di;
public class Main 
{
    public static void main(String[] args) 
    {
        Engine engine = new Engine();
        Car car = new Car(engine);
        car.stop();
    }
}

2. Setter Injection :
Dependency is injected using a setter method.

Example :
@Component
public class Car 
{
    private Engine engine;
    @Autowired
    public void setEngine(Engine engine) 
    {
        this.engine = engine;
    }
    public void drive() 
    {
        engine.start();
    }
}

3. Field Injection :
Dependency is injected directly into the field.

Example:
@Component
public class Car 
{
    @Autowired
    private Engine engine;
    public void drive() 
    {
        engine.start();
    }
}

Comparison :
| Feature              | Constructor Injection | Setter Injection | Field Injection |
| -------------------- | --------------------- | ---------------- | --------------- |
| Injection Through    | Constructor           | Setter Method    | Variable        |
| Dependency Mandatory | Yes                   | No               | No              |
| Easy Testing         | Yes                   | Moderate         | Difficult       |
| Recommended          | Yes                   | Sometimes        | No              |
| Immutability         | Yes                   | No               | No              |

